Most "best MCP servers" lists rank whatever has the most GitHub stars. That is a poor guide when you are wiring tools into a coding agent, because the question that matters is narrower: which servers change what your agent can actually do in a normal week of shipping software, and what do they cost you in setup, context tokens and risk? This roundup covers 15 servers that map to real developer jobs: source control, docs lookup, browser testing, error triage, databases, cloud, hosting, tickets, payments, design handoff, notes, containers and local machine control.

The short version: GitHub MCP is the best default for almost everyone, Context7 fixes stale-docs hallucinations on a free tier, Playwright MCP and Chrome DevTools MCP split the browser work between them, and Sentry plus Supabase or Postgres MCP close the loop from a production error to a database fix. Prices, limits and star counts below are as of November 27, 2026, and each one comes from the vendor's own page or the GitHub API on that date. We did not install or run these servers for this post, so where we describe behavior it comes from vendor docs, not our own testing.

{% youtube id="IBh3Wz5YIR8" title="What is the Chrome DevTools MCP server | Chrome for Developers" /%}

*Caption: Google's Chrome for Developers team explains the Chrome DevTools MCP server (pick 4 below). It is a quick primer on what an MCP server gives a coding agent beyond plain shell commands.*

## Quick picks

- **Best overall:** GitHub MCP. Official, MIT licensed, a hosted remote endpoint plus a local Docker option, and it covers the work most agents do every day.
- **Best free:** Context7 (1,000 API calls a month on the free plan) and Chrome DevTools MCP (open source, nothing to buy).
- **Best open source:** Chrome DevTools MCP (Apache-2.0, 59,605 stars) and Playwright MCP (Apache-2.0, 40,217 stars).
- **Best for debugging production:** Sentry MCP, paired with Supabase MCP or Postgres MCP when the fix lives in the database.
- **Best for teams that want isolation:** Docker MCP Toolkit, which runs servers in containers with documented CPU, memory and filesystem limits.
- **Best for design to code:** Figma Dev Mode MCP, as long as you read the per-seat rate limits first.
- **Highest blast radius:** Desktop Commander, Stripe MCP and Supabase MCP. Each can change real things, so use read-only modes, sandboxes and approvals.

If you want a Claude Code-specific ranking, read [AgentPedia's best MCP servers for Claude Code](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/best-mcp-servers-claude-code). If you are comparing the platforms that bundle many servers behind one connection, read [best MCP integration platforms in 2026](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/best-mcp-integration-platforms-2026). This post takes a different angle: it is client-agnostic, organized by developer job, and includes a works-with matrix for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Hermes and OpenCode. For adjacent categories see our posts on [memory MCP servers for Claude Code](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026) and [image and video MCP servers](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-image-and-video-mcp-servers-2026).

