Star counts, licenses and last-push dates came from the GitHub API on November 27, 2026. A star count measures attention, not quality, and several of these servers are hosted by their vendor, so the repo numbers describe a toolkit or client repo and not the hosted service you actually connect to.

## How to read the 15 sections

Each section follows the same order: what the server is, who it suits, what it does, pros, cons, benefits for a working developer, pricing and free tier, limits, a standout use case, a "skip it if" line and a setup snippet. Setup snippets are copied from the vendor's docs and trimmed. Config key names differ between clients (some use `mcpServers`, some use `servers`, Codex uses TOML), so treat each snippet as the shape of the config and check your client's own MCP docs for the exact file.

Two terms come up a lot. A **remote** server is hosted by the vendor, usually behind OAuth, and you connect with a URL. A **local** server is a process your client starts on your machine, usually through `npx` or Docker, and it uses whatever credentials you hand it. Remote servers are easier to set up and keep current. Local servers keep traffic on your machine but make you responsible for updates and for what the process can touch.

## The 15 servers, ranked

### 1. GitHub MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/github-mcp.png" name="GitHub MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** GitHub's official MCP server, MIT licensed, in the `github/github-mcp-server` repo (34,804 stars as of November 27, 2026; latest release v2.3.0 on November 24, 2026). You can use GitHub's hosted endpoint at `https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/` or run the server locally with Docker.

**Who it is for:** anyone whose code, issues and CI live on GitHub. If your agent writes code, this is the server that lets it read the ticket, look at the failing workflow and open the pull request without you pasting text between windows.

**Key features:**

- Tools are grouped into toolsets, and you pick which ones load with the `--toolsets` flag, the `X-MCP-Toolsets` header or a `/x/{toolset}` URL on the remote server. A default set loads if you name none.
- Read-only mode (`--read-only`, the `X-MCP-Readonly` header, or a `/readonly` URL) acts as a strict filter that takes precedence over everything else, so write tools stay off even if you list them by name.
- Lockdown mode (`--lockdown-mode` or the `X-MCP-Lockdown` header) is GitHub's guard for untrusted content in public repos.
- Authentication is OAuth on the remote server where your client supports it, or a personal access token otherwise.

**Pros:**

- Official, maintained daily (the repo was pushed on the day we checked).
- Fine-grained controls: toolsets, single tools, read-only and lockdown can be combined.
- A hosted endpoint means no Docker and no local process for most clients.
- GitHub's own docs say Copilot cloud agent starts with this server and Playwright MCP already configured, which tells you how central it is.

**Cons:**

- It acts with your account's rights. A broad token lets an agent push, merge or close things in every repo it can reach.
- Issues, PR comments and commit messages are untrusted text, which makes them a prompt injection path. Lockdown mode helps but GitHub does not call it a guarantee.
- GitHub Enterprise Server does not support the remote server, per the README. You run the local server instead.
- Every extra toolset adds tool definitions to the model's context on every request.

**Benefits for you:** fewer copy and paste steps between the agent and GitHub, and a safer starting point than a raw shell, because you can start with `repos,issues,pull_requests` read-only and widen only when you trust the workflow.

**Pricing and free tier (as of November 27, 2026):** the server repo is MIT and we found no separate fee for it. GitHub's docs say some tools depend on other subscriptions: Copilot cloud agent needs a Copilot subscription and code scanning needs GitHub Advanced Security. For reference, GitHub's Copilot plans page lists Free at $0, Pro at $10 per month, Pro+ at $39 per month and Max at $100 per month. We did not verify whether the hosted endpoint itself requires a paid Copilot plan, so check before you commit a team to it.

**Limits:** none documented beyond your own GitHub API rate limits and permissions.

**Standout use case:** point it at a failing Actions run, ask the agent to read the log, find the commit that broke it, and open a draft PR with the fix. Read-only for the investigation, then switch write tools on for the last step.

**Skip it if:** your code is not on GitHub, or the agent only needs local git history, where plain `git` is enough.

**Setup:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "url": "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/"
    }
  }
}
```

Append `/readonly` to that URL for a read-only connection, per the server's configuration guide. Your client's OAuth prompt handles sign-in; if it does not support OAuth, use a fine-grained personal access token with the narrowest scopes you can.

{% figure src="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/1/github/github-mcp-server" alt="GitHub repository card for github/github-mcp-server" caption="The github/github-mcp-server repository card. Source: GitHub" /%}

Related catalog skill: [github-code-review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/github-code-review) (by ruvnet) is a review workflow skill that pairs with GitHub access.

### 2. Context7

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/context7.png" name="Context7" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Upstash's documentation lookup server. It puts current, version-specific library docs and code examples into the prompt so the model stops writing against an API that changed after its training cutoff. The `upstash/context7` repo is MIT licensed with 65,551 stars as of November 27, 2026, and the latest release (`@upstash/context7-mcp@4.9.1`) was published on November 27, 2026.

**Who it is for:** anyone working with fast-moving frameworks, which is most of us.

**Key features:** the README documents two lookup tools, `resolve-library-id` and `query-docs`. You trigger it by ending a prompt with "use context7", or by letting a rule or skill do that for you. The hosted endpoint is `https://mcp.context7.com/mcp`, and `npx ctx7 setup` handles OAuth, generates an API key and installs either the MCP server or a CLI plus skill (no MCP needed). The `ctx7` CLI requires Node.js 22.13 or newer.

**Pros:**

- A tiny tool surface (two tools) means little context overhead.
- Free plan covers the MCP server, the CLI and every public library.
- CLI plus skill mode avoids loading MCP schemas at all.

**Cons:**

- Free accounts are blocked after 1,000 calls in a month, with 20 bonus calls per day until the reset.
- Private repositories need a paid plan (Pro bills $5 per 1M parsed tokens).
- Queries go to a hosted service, which some teams cannot allow.

**Benefits for you:** fewer hallucinated method names and deprecated flags, and no per-project docs crawling.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Free $0 with 1,000 API calls a month. Pro $10 per seat per month with 2,000 calls per seat, then $5 per 1,000 extra calls. Enterprise is custom.

**Standout use case:** "Set up the Next.js proxy file, use context7" against a framework version released after your model's cutoff.

**Skip it if:** your libraries are internal or pinned to old versions, or you cannot send queries to a hosted service.

**Setup:**

```bash
npx ctx7 setup
```

{% figure src="https://context7.com/opengraph-image?55026abe8d14202e" alt="Context7 website social card" caption="Context7's own Open Graph card from context7.com. Source: Context7 / Upstash" /%}

Related catalog skill: [context7-auto-research](https://agentskills.codes/skills/context7-auto-research) (by davila7) fetches current library docs through the Context7 API.

### 3. Playwright MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/playwright-mcp.png" name="Playwright MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Microsoft's MCP server for Playwright (`microsoft/playwright-mcp`, Apache-2.0, 40,217 stars as of November 27, 2026; latest release v0.0.86 on November 18, 2026). It lets an agent drive a real browser using structured accessibility snapshots instead of screenshots, so a plain text model can pick the next element.

**Who it is for:** developers who want the agent to open localhost, click through the flow it just built and report what broke.

**Key features:** needs Node.js 20 or newer. `--browser` accepts chrome, firefox, webkit and msedge. `--caps` turns on extra groups (vision, pdf, devtools). `--extension` connects to a running Chrome or Edge instance with the Playwright extension installed.

**Pros:**

- No vision model required; the snapshot is structured text.
- One-line setup in most clients: `claude mcp add playwright npx @playwright/mcp@latest`.
- The README lists setup for Claude Code, Cursor, Gemini CLI and opencode, and the repo's docs cover Codex.

**Cons:**

- Snapshots and tool schemas use a lot of context. Microsoft's own README says coding agents may do better with Playwright CLI plus skills because it is more token-efficient.
- Versions are still 0.0.x, so pin one in CI.
- `--allowed-origins` is documented as not a security boundary.

**Benefits for you:** the agent can verify its own UI work instead of telling you it is done.

**Pricing:** free and open source. No plan exists.

**Standout use case:** exploratory automation and self-healing tests, which the README says suit MCP better than the CLI.

**Skip it if:** the agent mostly runs scripted tests (use Playwright CLI and skills) or you need performance traces (use Chrome DevTools MCP).

**Setup:**

```bash
claude mcp add playwright npx @playwright/mcp@latest
```

Related catalog skill: [playwright-mcp](https://agentskills.codes/skills/playwright-mcp) (by sfc-gh-dflippo) covers browser testing and UI validation, with a focus on Streamlit apps.

### 4. Chrome DevTools MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/chrome-devtools-mcp.png" name="Chrome DevTools MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Google's server that lets a coding agent control and inspect a live Chrome browser (`ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp`, Apache-2.0, 59,605 stars as of November 27, 2026; latest release chrome-devtools-mcp-v1.14.0 on November 24, 2026). The README now brands it "Chrome DevTools for agents" and says it ships as both an MCP server and a CLI.

**Who it is for:** front-end developers chasing console errors, slow pages and network failures.

**Key features:** performance traces and insights, network request inspection, screenshots, and console messages with source-mapped stack traces. It uses Puppeteer for automation and needs a current Node.js LTS and current stable Chrome.

**Pros:**

- Performance and network depth that Playwright MCP does not target.
- Free and open source.
- Its README names Antigravity, Claude, Cursor and Copilot as example clients, and the repo mentions Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI setup.

**Cons:**

- Officially supports Google Chrome and Chrome for Testing only.
- Data collection of usage statistics is on by default (opt out with `--no-usage-statistics`).
- Performance tools may send trace URLs to the Google CrUX API (disable with `--no-performance-crux`).
- The README warns that it exposes browser content to the client, so avoid sensitive pages.

**Benefits for you:** one agent session can reproduce a bug, read the console, record a trace and suggest the fix.

**Pricing:** free and open source.

**Standout use case:** "Why is localhost:8080 loading slowly?" followed by a trace and a code change.

**Skip it if:** you only need cross-browser flows (use Playwright MCP) or you test in Firefox or Safari.

**Setup:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "chrome-devtools": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest", "--no-usage-statistics"]
    }
  }
}
```

{% x id="1970505063064825994" /%}

{% x id="1970503277621256263" /%}

Related catalog skill: [chrome-devtools](https://agentskills.codes/skills/chrome-devtools) (by mrgoonie) is a Puppeteer CLI script skill for browser automation and performance analysis. It is a skill, not this MCP server, so pick it if you prefer scripts over a server.

### 5. Sentry MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/sentry-mcp.png" name="Sentry MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Sentry's MCP service for searching errors, analyzing performance, triaging issues, reading documentation and managing projects. The code lives in the `getsentry/toolkit` repo (975 stars as of November 27, 2026, a repo that also holds the Sentry CLI; GitHub does not detect a license). The remote server is at `https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp`.

**Who it is for:** teams already on Sentry who want the agent to start from the stack trace.

**Key features:** all connections use OAuth. You can scope a connection to an organization (`/mcp/{organizationSlug}`) or a single project (`/mcp/{organizationSlug}/{projectSlug}`), and Sentry recommends project scope when possible. A local stdio option runs through the Sentry CLI (`sentry mcp`) using your CLI login. The older `npx @sentry/mcp-server` package is described as legacy.

**Pros:**

- Project scoping narrows what the agent sees.
- Remote OAuth means no token in config.
- Local CLI mode supports self-hosted Sentry.

**Cons:**

- The repo says its tool selection targets human-in-the-loop coding agents, not fully autonomous ones.
- Seer, the AI debugging agent, needs a paid subscription.
- We did not find a documented read-only switch, so scope by project and review tool calls.

**Benefits for you:** paste nothing; ask for the top unresolved issue in the project and the agent pulls the event.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Developer $0 (one user, lists MCP access), Team $26 per month, Business $80 per month (both when billed annually with default pre-paid data), Enterprise custom.

**Standout use case:** "Find the newest unresolved error in checkout, read the stack trace and open a fix."

**Skip it if:** you do not use Sentry, or you need a documented read-only mode.

**Setup:**

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http sentry https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp/{organizationSlug}/{projectSlug}
```

{% figure src="https://mcp.sentry.dev/flow.jpg" alt="Sentry MCP flow diagram from mcp.sentry.dev" caption="Open Graph image from Sentry's MCP site. Source: Sentry" /%}

Related catalog skill: [sentry](https://agentskills.codes/skills/sentry) (by openai) inspects Sentry issues and events.

### 6. Supabase MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/supabase-mcp.png" name="Supabase MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Supabase's official server for managing projects and querying the database from an agent (`supabase/mcp`, Apache-2.0, 3,025 stars as of November 27, 2026; latest release mcp-server-supabase-v0.21.0 on November 26, 2026). The hosted endpoint is `https://mcp.supabase.com/mcp`.

**Who it is for:** teams on Supabase who want schema, migrations and logs in the agent loop.

**Key features:** tools come in feature groups. The docs list database tools (including `apply_migration` and `execute_sql`), debugging tools (`query_logs` and `get_advisors`), docs search (`search_docs`) and more. Branching tools are experimental and need a paid plan. The setup panel lets you pick a project, switch on read-only mode and choose feature groups; the same switches exist as URL parameters (`project_ref`, `read_only=true`). Sign-in is OAuth, no personal access token needed. Running Supabase locally exposes the server at `http://localhost:54321/mcp`.

**Pros:**

- Project scoping and a read-only option (`read_only=true`, which runs queries as a read-only Postgres user) are built into the setup.
- Advisors give the agent security and performance hints.
- The docs page shows an update date of November 18, 2026.

**Cons:**

- The docs open with a warning that connecting an LLM to your projects carries security risks.
- If you select no project, all projects are accessible.
- `execute_sql` and `apply_migration` can change data and schema.

**Benefits for you:** the agent writes a migration, applies it to a dev project and checks the advisors before you review it.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Free $0 (2 active projects, paused after 1 week of inactivity, 500 MB database). Pro from $25 per month. The MCP server adds no separate fee.

**Standout use case:** debugging a failing query with `query_logs` and `get_advisors`, then applying the fix to a branch.

**Skip it if:** you are not on Supabase, or you cannot keep the agent away from production data (point it at a dev project and use read-only).

**Setup:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "supabase": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.supabase.com/mcp?project_ref=<your-project-ref>&read_only=true"
    }
  }
}
```

The URL parameters come from Supabase's docs. Your client may want a slightly different key layout, so check its MCP docs.

Related catalog skills: [supabase-postgres-best-practices](https://agentskills.codes/skills/supabase-postgres-best-practices) (by davila7) for query and schema work, and [supabase](https://agentskills.codes/skills/supabase) (by alinaqi) for CLI, migrations and RLS.

### 7. Postgres MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/postgres-mcp.png" name="Postgres MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** this slot goes to Postgres MCP Pro from Crystal DBA (`crystaldba/postgres-mcp`, MIT, 3,503 stars as of November 27, 2026), not the Anthropic-era reference server. The original Postgres reference server now lives in `modelcontextprotocol/servers-archived`, which GitHub marks as archived (last push May 28, 2025), and its npm package carries a deprecation notice reading "Archived and unmaintained; no security guarantees." Do not start new work on that one.

**Who it is for:** developers who want an agent to tune queries on a real Postgres database, not just run SQL.

**Key features:** database health checks (index health, connection utilization, vacuum health and more), index tuning, EXPLAIN plan review with hypothetical index simulation, and SQL execution with two access modes: unrestricted (read and write, for development) and restricted (read-only transactions plus an execution time limit, suggested for production). Index tuning needs the `pg_stat_statements` and `hypopg` extensions.

**Pros:**

- Restricted mode is built in.
- Goes beyond raw queries into performance advice.
- Runs locally under your own credentials.

**Cons:**

- The latest GitHub release is v0.3.0 from May 16, 2025, though the repo was pushed in the last day.
- Index tuning needs extensions your database may not have.
- Unrestricted mode can write.

**Benefits for you:** "why is this query slow" becomes an EXPLAIN plus an index suggestion you can test.

**Pricing:** free and open source.

**Standout use case:** a slow-query review on a staging copy with restricted mode on.

**Skip it if:** you are on Supabase and want its managed tools, or you only need read access to a small database.

**Setup:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "postgres": {
      "command": "docker",
      "args": ["run", "-i", "--rm", "-e", "DATABASE_URI", "crystaldba/postgres-mcp", "--access-mode=restricted"],
      "env": { "DATABASE_URI": "postgresql://username:password@localhost:5432/dbname" }
    }
  }
}
```

{% figure src="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/1/crystaldba/postgres-mcp" alt="GitHub repository card for crystaldba/postgres-mcp" caption="The crystaldba/postgres-mcp repository card. Source: GitHub" /%}

Related catalog skill: [postgres-patterns](https://agentskills.codes/skills/postgres-patterns) (by affaan-m) covers query optimization, schema design and indexing.

### 8. Cloudflare MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/cloudflare-mcp.png" name="Cloudflare MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Cloudflare's managed MCP servers, including the Cloudflare API MCP server at `https://mcp.cloudflare.com/mcp`. Cloudflare's docs say it covers over 2,500 API endpoints across DNS, Workers, R2, Zero Trust and more, plus the developer docs. The docs page was last updated October 27, 2026.

**Who it is for:** developers who deploy on Cloudflare and want the agent to manage it.

**Key features:** instead of exposing every endpoint as a tool, the server offers `search()` and `execute()` tools to find and call endpoints, and `docs()` to search docs. It uses a Code Mode pattern where the model writes JavaScript against a typed OpenAPI representation, run inside an isolated sandbox. OAuth lets you choose permissions, and a bearer API token works for CI.

**Pros:**

- Token cost: Cloudflare says Code Mode uses about 1,000 tokens regardless of endpoint count (vendor-reported), against about 1,170,000 for native MCP with full schemas for 2,594 tools.
- One server instead of many.
- Product-specific managed servers (docs, containers, browser rendering and more) also exist if you want narrower scope.

**Cons:**

- A wide API behind one `execute()` call is a large blast radius. Pick narrow OAuth permissions.
- The `mcp-server-cloudflare` repo (4,499 stars, Apache-2.0) was last pushed October 22, 2026, so check the docs page for what is current.
- Needs a Cloudflare account and permission choices up front, and we found no documented read-only switch.

**Benefits for you:** one connection for DNS, Workers and storage chores that otherwise mean dashboard clicking.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** we found no MCP fee. The platform itself has a Workers Free plan and a Workers Paid plan with a $5 per month minimum.

**Standout use case:** "List my Workers and show which have no recent traffic."

**Skip it if:** you do not use Cloudflare, or you cannot grant scoped permissions.

**Setup:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "cloudflare-api": { "url": "https://mcp.cloudflare.com/mcp" }
  }
}
```

{% figure src="https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/model-context-protocol/cloudflare/servers-for-cloudflare/og.png?v=2698c333db6912a5" alt="Cloudflare docs card for Cloudflare's own MCP servers" caption="Open Graph image from Cloudflare's MCP server docs. Source: Cloudflare" /%}

Related catalog skill: [cloudflare-manager](https://agentskills.codes/skills/cloudflare-manager) (by qdhenry) covers Workers, KV, R2, Pages, DNS and routes.

### 9. Vercel MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/vercel-mcp.png" name="Vercel MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Vercel's official remote MCP server at `https://mcp.vercel.com`, with OAuth. Vercel's docs say it is available on all plans, and that connecting it gives the AI tool the same access as your Vercel user account.

**Who it is for:** teams that deploy on Vercel and want the agent to look at deployments and logs without opening the dashboard.

**Key features:** search and navigate Vercel documentation, manage projects and deployments, analyze deployment logs, and query visitors, page views and custom events. A Vercel changelog entry dated November 24, 2026 says agents can now manage AI Gateway through the server too: virtual models, routing rules and spending budgets, with team-level access and the required permissions. The docs list setup for Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, VS Code, Gemini CLI, OpenCode and others, plus an `add-mcp` helper: `npx -y add-mcp https://mcp.vercel.com -g`.

**Pros:**

- Remote OAuth with no local process.
- Requires explicit user consent for each client connection, which Vercel describes as protection against confused deputy attacks.
- Broadest client list in the docs of any server in this post.

**Cons:**

- Same access as your user account, so a careless prompt can touch every project you can.
- Hosted only; we found no open source repo for the server itself (Vercel's `mcp-handler` repo is a library for building your own).
- Docs say the tool list you see depends on your server configuration, your client's OAuth grant and your team permissions, so two people can see different tools.

**Benefits for you:** "why did the last deployment fail" becomes a log read inside the same session that wrote the code.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Hobby $0, Pro $20 per month (includes $20 of usage credit). Vercel MCP itself is available on all plans, with use subject to Vercel's AI Product Terms.

**Standout use case:** diagnose a failed deploy from build logs and push the fix.

**Skip it if:** you do not host on Vercel.

**Setup:**

```bash
npx -y add-mcp https://mcp.vercel.com -g
```

Related catalog skill: [mcp-integration](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-integration) (by anthropics) explains how to add and configure MCP servers in `.mcp.json`, which applies to any server in this list.

### 10. Linear MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/linear-mcp.png" name="Linear MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Linear's hosted MCP server at `https://mcp.linear.app/mcp`, built on Streamable HTTP with OAuth 2.1 and dynamic client registration. Linear's docs describe tools for finding, creating and updating objects such as issues, projects and comments.

**Who it is for:** teams that track work in Linear and want the agent to create and update tickets while it codes.

**Key features:** read-write access by default. For read-only you have three options per Linear's docs: choose read-only on the consent screen, connect to `https://mcp.linear.app/mcp/readonly`, or request only the read OAuth scope. Authentication can also use a bearer token or Linear API key. When you authenticate you can scope the connection to a subset of teams (workspace data stays visible). Linear's docs mention Claude and Cursor and include `codex mcp add linear --url https://mcp.linear.app/mcp` for Codex.

**Pros:**

- A documented read-only endpoint.
- Team scoping.
- Enterprise-managed authorization with Okta is documented.

**Cons:**

- Hosted only. We found no public MCP source repo (the `linear/linear` repo is Linear's SDK and tooling, 1,691 stars).
- Codex users may need to enable the `rmcp` client feature in `~/.codex/config.toml` the first time, per Linear's note.
- Team scoping does not hide workspace data.

**Benefits for you:** turn a spec into a parent issue and sub-issues without leaving the terminal.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Free $0 (unlimited members, 2 teams, 250 issues), Basic $10 per user per month, Business $16 per user per month, both billed yearly. The MCP server has no separate listed fee.

**Standout use case:** "Read this PRD, create a project and the issues to deliver it."

**Skip it if:** your tracker is Jira or GitHub Issues (GitHub MCP already covers that).

**Setup:**

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http linear https://mcp.linear.app/mcp
```

Related catalog skill: [linear](https://agentskills.codes/skills/linear) (by lobehub) is an issue management guide for creating issues, updating status and adding comments.

### 11. Stripe MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/stripe-mcp.png" name="Stripe MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** Stripe's remote MCP server at `https://mcp.stripe.com`. Stripe's docs label it Public preview and say it provides tools for interacting with the Stripe API and searching the Stripe knowledge base, including documentation and support articles. The code lives in Stripe's `stripe/ai` toolkit repo (MIT, 2,101 stars as of November 27, 2026).

**Who it is for:** developers building on Stripe who want the agent to look up API behavior and work in a sandbox.

**Key features:** Stripe recommends agent plugins, installed through the Stripe CLI (`stripe agent setup`), which configure the server and install Stripe's skills. Manual setup is documented for several clients, including Cursor, Claude Code and Codex. OAuth consent can grant different permissions per live account or sandbox. For autonomous clients, Stripe says to use an agent API key with only the permissions the client needs.

**Pros:**

- Sandbox and live permissions are set separately.
- Admins can switch MCP access on or off for the whole team, separately for live and sandbox.
- Sessions are listed and revocable in the Dashboard.

**Cons:**

- Public preview, so behavior can change.
- OAuth refresh tokens expire after 14 days, and refreshing does not extend the session beyond 14 days from the first authorization. You reconnect.
- Starting January 11, 2027, Stripe MCP will stop accepting full-access secret keys and non-agent restricted API keys. Migrate to an agent API key or OAuth before then.

**Benefits for you:** the agent can check the real API shape and test against a sandbox instead of guessing parameter names.

**Pricing:** we found no MCP fee on Stripe's docs page. Stripe's own transaction pricing applies to what you process and was not part of this check.

**Standout use case:** build a checkout flow in a sandbox account with the docs search tool open next to it.

**Skip it if:** you do not use Stripe, or you cannot keep the agent on a sandbox and away from live-mode money movement.

**Setup:**

```bash
stripe agent setup
```

Install the Stripe CLI first, following Stripe's docs for your operating system.

Related catalog skill: [stripe-integration](https://agentskills.codes/skills/stripe-integration) (by wshobson) covers checkout, subscriptions and webhooks.

### 12. Figma Dev Mode MCP

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/figma-dev-mode-mcp.png" name="Figma Dev Mode MCP" size=40 /%}

**What it is:** the Figma MCP server, which brings Figma into your AI workflow. Figma's developer docs say it gives agents design context to generate code from your files and, on the remote server, lets agents write native content back to the canvas. The remote server lives at `https://mcp.figma.com/mcp`. A desktop server also exists, which runs through the Figma desktop app and is intended for specific organization and enterprise needs. The `figma/mcp-server-guide` repo has 2,242 stars and no detected license.

**Who it is for:** teams turning Figma frames into front-end code.

**Key features:** generate code from selected frames, extract variables, components and layout data, retrieve FigJam content, and write to the canvas (Figma says writing outside your drafts needs a Full seat, and it is free during the beta but will eventually be a usage-based paid feature). Figma's client table lists Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and Gemini CLI, among others.

**Pros:**

- Official, with documented support across many clients.
- Works with Code Connect for design system mapping.
- Remote setup is the recommended path.

**Cons:**

- Rate limits are tight. Figma's table: Starter plan 20 tool calls per month; View or Collab seat 20 per month; Full or Dev seat on Professional 200 per day (10 per minute); Organization 200 per day (15 per minute); Enterprise up to 600 per day (20 per minute).
- Big frames are slow, and Figma's docs advise avoiding selecting large, heavy frames.
- The desktop server needs a Dev or Full seat on a paid plan.

**Benefits for you:** the generated code starts from your real variables and components, not a screenshot.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Dev seat $12 per month on Professional, $25 on Organization, $35 on Enterprise; Full seat $16, $55 and $90; Collab seat $3, $5 and $5 (Figma's pricing page; Organization and Enterprise are billed annually). The MCP server is included with the seats above subject to the call limits.

**Standout use case:** implement a selected frame as a component using your design tokens.

**Skip it if:** your team is on the Starter plan (20 calls a month will not last a day), or your designs are not in Figma.

**Setup:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "figma": { "url": "https://mcp.figma.com/mcp" }
  }
}
```

