```

Related catalog skill: [notion-spec-to-implementation](https://agentskills.codes/skills/notion-spec-to-implementation) (by openai) turns Notion specs into plans, tasks and progress tracking.

### 14. Docker MCP Toolkit

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/docker-mcp-toolkit.png" name="Docker MCP Toolkit" size=40 /%}


**What it is:** a management layer in Docker Desktop for running containerized MCP servers in profiles and connecting them to agents. Docker's docs mark it Beta and describe the interface in Docker Desktop 4.62 and later. The open source piece, `docker/mcp-gateway`, is MIT with 1,651 stars as of November 27, 2026.

**Who it is for:** teams that want many MCP servers with a consistent security posture and a single connection point.

**Key features:** a catalog to browse and launch servers, profiles as named collections (one per project or environment), a gateway that clients connect to, and Dynamic MCP, which lets an agent search the catalog and add servers during a conversation. Docker's docs list setup for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini and OpenCode.

**Pros:**

- Docker's docs say images under `mcp/` in the catalog are built and digitally signed by Docker, each with an SBOM.
- Runtime limits: each tool container is restricted to 1 CPU and 2 GB of memory, and by default has no host filesystem access.
- Docker says requests containing sensitive information such as secrets are intercepted and blocked.

**Cons:**

- Beta, and it is built around Docker Desktop.
- Dynamic MCP lets agents add servers on their own, so review that setting for your team.
- It adds a layer between you and each server, which is one more thing to debug.

**Benefits for you:** one gateway config for every client, with limits you do not have to build.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026):** Docker Personal $0, Pro $9 per user per month (annual; $11 monthly), Team $15 ($16 monthly), Business $24 (annual only). We did not verify which plans include the Toolkit beta, so check Docker's pricing page for your plan.

**Standout use case:** a team standard of signed servers in a shared profile, with read-only filesystem defaults.

**Skip it if:** you do not use Docker Desktop, or you need one hosted server only (a remote URL is simpler).

**Setup:** in Docker Desktop, open Settings, choose Beta features, enable Docker MCP Toolkit, then create a profile and add servers from the catalog.

Related catalog skill: [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) (by anthropics) guides building MCP servers, useful if you plan to package your own for the catalog.

### 15. Desktop Commander

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-mcp-servers-for-developers-2026/logos/desktop-commander.png" name="Desktop Commander" size=40 /%}


**What it is:** a local MCP server that lets an agent run terminal commands and manage files (`wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP`, MIT, 10,443 stars as of November 27, 2026; latest release v0.3.4 on November 18, 2026). It lives under an individual's GitHub account, not a large vendor's.

**Who it is for:** developers using a chat client with no built-in shell, such as Claude Desktop, who want it to run commands and edit files.

**Key features:** long-running terminal commands with process control, interactive sessions (SSH, databases, dev servers), in-memory code execution (Python, Node.js, R), full filesystem operations with surgical text edits, Excel, PDF and DOCX support, and local tool-call history through `get_recent_tool_calls`. The README lists setup for Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf and Codex, including `codex mcp add desktop-commander -- npx -y @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest`. A Remote MCP option exists for ChatGPT and Claude web.

**Pros:**

- Gives a chat client terminal and file reach.
- Local history of recent tool calls through `get_recent_tool_calls`.
- Call history stays on your machine.

**Cons:**

- The README itself says its guardrails (symlink prevention, a command blocklist) are "not a sandbox." An agent with this server can do what your user can do.
- Overlaps heavily with what Claude Code, Codex and similar CLIs already do natively.
- It is community maintained, not vendor backed, so judge the support risk.

**Benefits for you:** a terminal and filesystem for clients that have none.

**Pricing:** free and open source.

**Standout use case:** analyzing a CSV or Excel file in a chat client by having the agent run code locally.

**Skip it if:** you already use a coding CLI with its own shell and file tools. The README offers Docker installation if you want isolation.

**Setup:**

```bash
npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest setup
```

