## Feature matrix

"Yes" means we found it in the vendor's docs or README. "Partial" means a limited or indirect version exists. "No" means we did not find it in the docs we read, which can mean it does not exist or that we missed it. "Not verified" means we could not confirm it either way.

| Server | Hosted remote | Local option | OAuth sign-in | Read-only or limited mode | Open source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GitHub MCP | Yes | Yes (Docker) | Yes | Yes (read-only and lockdown modes) | Yes |
| Context7 | Yes | Yes (npx) | Partial (CLI setup uses OAuth, API key for the server) | Partial (lookup tools only) | Yes (client and CLI) |
| Playwright MCP | No | Yes | No | No | Yes |
| Chrome DevTools MCP | No | Yes | No | No | Yes |
| Sentry MCP | Yes | Yes (via Sentry CLI) | Yes | No | Partial (toolkit repo) |
| Supabase MCP | Yes | Yes (local CLI, self-hosted) | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Postgres MCP | No | Yes | No | Yes (restricted mode) | Yes |
| Cloudflare MCP | Yes | No | Yes | No documented switch (scope OAuth permissions) | Partial |
| Vercel MCP | Yes | No | Yes | Not verified | No |
| Linear MCP | Yes | No (mcp-remote proxy for older clients) | Yes | Yes (read-only endpoint) | No |
| Stripe MCP | Yes | Not verified | Yes | Partial (per-environment permissions, agent API keys) | Partial (toolkit repo) |
| Figma Dev Mode MCP | Yes | Yes (desktop app server) | Not verified | No | Partial (guide repo only) |
| Notion MCP | Yes | Partial (legacy repo, unmaintained) | Yes | Not verified | Partial |
| Docker MCP Toolkit | No | Yes | Not verified | Partial (no host filesystem by default) | Partial (gateway MIT) |
| Desktop Commander | Partial (Remote MCP option) | Yes | No | No | Yes |

## Works-with matrix: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Hermes, OpenCode

"Documented" means the vendor's own docs or README names the client. "Standard transport" means the vendor does not name the client, but the client's own MCP docs list the transport the server uses, so it should connect. We did not test any of these pairings. All six clients document support for local stdio servers and remote HTTP servers with OAuth: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, OpenCode and Hermes all say so in their own MCP docs, so a "Standard transport" cell is a reasonable expectation, not a promise.

| Server | Claude Code | Codex | Cursor | Gemini CLI | Hermes | OpenCode |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GitHub MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Documented |
| Context7 | Documented | Documented (Codex docs use it as an example) | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Documented |
| Playwright MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Documented |
| Chrome DevTools MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Sentry MCP | Documented | Standard transport | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Supabase MCP | Documented | Standard transport | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Postgres MCP | Standard transport | Standard transport | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Cloudflare MCP | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Vercel MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Documented |
| Linear MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Stripe MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport | Standard transport |
| Figma Dev Mode MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Documented |
| Notion MCP | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Documented | Standard transport |
| Docker MCP Toolkit | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Documented |
| Desktop Commander | Documented | Documented | Documented | Documented | Standard transport | Standard transport |

Three caveats. First, Cloudflare's docs name Claude and a general OAuth flow for its MCP server, but the other client names on its page refer to its skills plugin, so those cells are "Standard transport". Second, "Documented" on a page only tells you the vendor wrote setup steps, not that every feature works equally well in that client (Figma notes that some canvas features are supported by select clients only). Third, Hermes is "Documented" only for Notion, the one vendor page we found that names it. Hermes's own docs say it accepts local stdio servers and remote HTTP servers (including OAuth) in one config and supports per-server tool filtering through a `tools.include` list, which helps with servers that expose many tools.

## Head-to-head: the closest pairs

### Playwright MCP vs Chrome DevTools MCP: which to pick

Pick **Playwright MCP** when you need to click through a flow in Chrome, Firefox, WebKit or Edge and check the result. Pick **Chrome DevTools MCP** when you need console errors, network requests or a performance trace from a real Chrome. They do not compete as much as they overlap: both can open a page and take a snapshot, but Playwright's draw is cross-browser flows and Chrome DevTools' draw is depth in one browser. Playwright's `--caps devtools` option adds some DevTools-style tools, though we did not compare their depth with Chrome DevTools MCP. If you can only add one and you build front-end features, start with Playwright MCP; if you chase performance bugs, start with Chrome DevTools MCP. Privacy differs: Chrome DevTools MCP collects usage statistics by default (opt out with a flag), while we found no such default in Playwright MCP's README.

### Supabase MCP vs Postgres MCP: which to pick

Pick **Supabase MCP** if your database is on Supabase: it covers migrations, logs, advisors and branching and has a read-only option in the setup panel, but the docs warn about security risks and say that with no project selected all projects are reachable. Pick **Postgres MCP** (Crystal DBA's) if your Postgres lives anywhere else or you want index tuning and EXPLAIN analysis: restricted mode gives read-only transactions with a time limit, and it runs locally under your credentials.

### Hosted servers vs Docker MCP Toolkit: which to pick

Pick **individual hosted servers** (GitHub, Linear, Sentry, Vercel, Notion) when the vendor runs the server and you want OAuth and no local process. Pick **Docker MCP Toolkit** when you run local servers and want one gateway, signed images, CPU and memory limits and no host filesystem by default. It is Beta and tied to Docker Desktop, so for a team with strict controls, test it in a pilot profile first. For most solo developers, three or four hosted servers with read-only settings are less to maintain than a gateway.

